 { In this endgame, the pawn structure speaks clearly in White's favour: the protected passed pawn on b5 is a constant headache for Black, who also suffers from weak points at d5 and d6. However, for the moment Salov has everything defended, and it is evident that White should try to add some complexity to the position. } 1. { -KEY- } Rf1 $1 { Preparing the opening of a new front on the f-file. } Rb6 ( { Here and later } 1... Be6 { was possible in order to swap the active enemy bishop, although after } 2. e4 Bxd5 3. Rxd5 { followed by Ke3 and f3-f4 or g3-g4, White keeps a clear initiative. Interestingly, Salov had declined the exchange of bishops on d5 a few moves earlier, whereas Sokolov in his 'Chess Informant' notes suggested it. } ) 
2. Ke1 ( 2. e4 { or } 2. Ke2 { seemed more natural, but Sokolov is not in a hurry, aware that the opponent can't undertake anything active. } ) 
Rf8 3. Ke2 Rbb8 4. e4 Bc8 5. Ke3 Rb6 6. Rdf2 $1 { White has deployed his forces in an ideal way, and now waits for the right moment to clear the f-file. } Bd7 ( { In the event of } 6... f6 { Dautov indicates the sacrifice } 7. g4 $1 hxg4 8. Rh1 { when both } gxf3 ( { and } 8... Be6 9. fxg4 Bxd5 10. cxd5 { followed by Kd3-c4 } ) 
9. Rh7 Kd8 10. gxf3 Bd7 11. Rfh2 { preparing Rg7 and Rhh7, clearly favour White. } ) 
7. g4 $1 { The right pawn, so as to clear two files in one strike. } ( 7. f4 $6 f6 { is less convincing; e.g. } 8. f5 g5 9. Rh1 Rh8 10. g4 Be8 11. g3 Rh6 12. Rfh2 Rh8 13. gxh5 Rh6 14. g4 Bf7 15. Bxf7 Kxf7 16. Rd2 Ke7 17. Rhd1 Rh8 18. Rh1 Rh6 { and Black holds - Dautov. } ) 
hxg4 ( { Or } 7... h4 8. g5 { with ideas like 9.f4 and 9.Rh1 - Sokolov. } ) 
8. fxg4 Be6 ( { Seeing that } 8... f6 $6 9. g5 f5 10. Rh1 { would be very unpleasant for him, Salov finally accepts the exchange of bishops. } ) 
9. g5 { Fixing a target on f7. } Rbb8 10. Rf6 $1 { Provoking the capture on d5. } Bxd5 ( { A waiting move like } 10... Rbc8 { runs into } 11. Bxe6 fxe6 12. Rxf8 Rxf8 13. Rxf8 Kxf8 14. b6 { , when Black's king is too far away from the b-pawn, as observed by Gobyaya. } ) 
11. cxd5 $1 { The correct recapture so as to enable a future entrance of the king on the queenside. } Rb7 12. Kd3 Rd7 13. Kc4 Rb7 14. R1f3 ( { The immediate } 14. Rh1 { was also strong. } ) 
Rbb8 15. Rh3 $1 { The last straw - in order to resist the invasion of the enemy rook, Black will have to abandon defensive tasks on other parts of the board. This is indeed the essence of the 'new front' theme. } Rh8 ( { Or } 15... Rb6 16. Rh7 Ke8 17. g3 $1 { with a decisive zugzwang - Sokolov; e.g. } Ke7 18. Rxg6 ) 
16. Rxh8 Rxh8 17. b6 $1 { Of course; with credit to White's 11th move. } Rh1 18. Kb5 c4 ( { Seeing that } 18... Rb1 19. Ka6 { is hopeless for him, Salov opts for drastic measures. } ) 
19. b7 ( { Evidently, } 19. Kxc4 $4 Rb1 { would ruin all White's previous efforts - Dautov. } ) 
c3 20. Ka6 Rb1 ( { Or } 20... c2 21. b8=Q $11 c1=Q $11 22. Qb7 { with a quick mate. } ) 
21. Rf3 c2 22. Rc3 { With Ka7 and b7-b8Q coming up, Black resigned. } 
 1-0

