 { Black is better thanks to his more active rook and bishop, but obviously it is no big advantage. To win such positions you usually need some mistakes from your opponent, so your task is to create the opportunities for him! } 1... { -KEY- } g5 $1 { Fixing the h3-pawn as a future target while preparing ...Kg7-f6-e5. } ( { Less precise was } 1... Kg7 $6 2. h4 $1 ) 
2. Ke2 Kg7 3. g3 $6 ( { a) A natural choice; so as to meet } 3. g3 Kf6 { with the restrictive } 4. f4 { but now the kingside structure becomes less stable. } ) 
 ( { b) Active defence with } 3. Rb2 Bc6 4. Rb6 $1 { was preferable; e.g. } Be8 ( { or } 4. g3 Rxa2 5. Ke3 { recovering the pawn at once; } ) 
5. Bc4 $1 { (both here and on the next move, the a6-pawn is poisoned) } Kf6 6. Kd2 $1 { heading for c2-b2, when Black has nothing better than } Bb5 { simplifying to a draw. } ) 
Bc6 $1 4. Rb2 ( { a) Due to Black's previous move, } 4. f4 $2 { fails here to } gxf4 5. gxf4 Bb5 $1 6. Bxb5 axb5 7. h4 Rh3 ) 
 ( { b) But the active } 4. Rc2 $5 { was possible; e.g. } Ba4 5. Rc5 $1 Rxa2 6. Ke3 h6 7. Ra5 { with an impending draw. } ) 
Kf6 ( { Now how should White cope with the threat of } 5... Ke5 { - ? } ) 
5. Kd2 $2 { A useless move provoked by time trouble. } ( { a) } 5. f4 $2 { was equally bad due to } gxf4 6. gxf4 Bb5 7. Bxb5 Rxh3 $1 { and Black wins a pawn. } ) 
 ( { b) On the other hand, } 5. Ke3 { seems rather natural. However, Black could lose a tempo by } Bd7 $1 { exploiting the fact that White has no solid reply. } ( 5. Kd2 Bb5 6. Rb3 { is unconvincing; } ) 
 ( { while } 5. Kd2 Ke5 { can be met by } 6. f4 ) 
 { There could follow } 6. Kd2 ( { not } 6. Rc2 $2 Bb5 ) 
h5 $1 { (working on a new front) } 7. Ke3 ( { or } 7. h4 $6 g4 ) 
h4 8. gxh4 gxh4 9. f4 e5 $1 10. f5 Bb5 11. Rb3 Rxa2 12. Bxb5 axb5 13. Kf3 Kg5 $1 14. Rxb5 Ra3 { and 15...Kf4 next, with good winning prospects. } ) 
 ( { c) Thus White's best chance is probably } 5. Rb6 { when Black could reply } Be8 $1 6. Bc4 a5 { followed by h7-h5, just as before. Contrary to the 3.Rb2 subline, here } 7. Kd2 Ke5 8. Kc2 $2 { runs into } Rf3 $1 { when the defects of 3.g3 make themselves remembered. } ) 
Ke5 6. Rb6 ( { Or } 6. f3 f5 $1 7. exf5 exf5 { with strong pressure. } ) 
Bxe4 7. Bxa6 Rxa2+ 8. Ke3 { In a pure rook ending or bishop ending, White would have good drawing chances in such a structure; but with both of them on the board, he won't be able to parry the attack on the h3-pawn. } Ra3+ 9. Ke2 Bf3+ ( { Another good option was } 9... Bf5 { softening up that pawn for the rook. } ) 
10. Kd2 Bg2 11. Bd3 h5 $1 { Pawn trades usually favour the defender, and this is no exception. } 12. h4 gxh4 13. gxh4 Ra4 { Conquering the h-pawn, with an easy win. } ( { There followed } 13... Ra4 14. Rb5 Bd5 15. Rb8 Rxh4 16. Be2 Rh1 17. Ke3 h4 18. Rh8 Bg2 19. Rh5 f5 20. f3 h3 21. Rh6 Rc1 22. Kd2 Rc6 23. Ke3 Rc3 24. Kf2 Rc2 25. Ke3 { and the rest of this endgame was covered inExample 418 at the beginning of Chapter Seven. } ) 

 0-1

