 { White is evidently better in this rook ending, but the weak pawns on a6 and c6 alone won't be enough for victory. } 1. { -KEY- } g4 $1 { A key advance in order to open a new front on the kingside. Note that on most other moves Black could reply 1...h5!, gaining some space and avoiding a future target on the h-file, an idea that we are familiar with from the 'Space' section in Chapter Six. } h6 ( { a) The active } 1... c5 $2 { backfires after } 2. Kd3 Kc6 3. Kc4 { - Stohl. } ) 
 ( { b) While } 1... h5 $5 { is well met by } 2. gxh5 gxh5 3. f4 $1 { intending f4xe5+ and Ke4. } ) 
2. h4 Ra8 { Awaiting White's next step. } ( { Instead, active defence with } 2... f5 $2 { would just help the opponent after } 3. gxf5 gxf5 4. f4 $1 { when the f5-pawn turns into a target. } ) 
3. b4 Ra7 4. a3 { An ideal structure in such positions, reinforcing White's control of the a5- and c5-squares. } Ra8 5. Kd3 Ra7 6. e3 Ra8 7. f4 $1 { Nikolic resumes the work on the new front. } ( { After } 7. f4 { there is now the threat of } -- 8. fxe5 fxe5 9. Ke4 ) 
exf4 8. exf4 Kc7 { Black prepares ...Kb6 and ...Rd8+ in order to activate his rook, but White is first to create new threats on the kingside. } 9. h5 $1 { This advance is bound to create some weakness in the enemy camp. Note that, from a5, the rook is able to act on both flanks at the same time. } gxh5 ( { a) After } 9... g5 10. Ke4 { the king invades via f5-g6 with decisive effect. } ) 
 ( { b) While the sacrifice } 9... Rg8 { makes little sense after } 10. Rxa6 gxh5 11. gxh5 Rg4 12. Ke4 Rh4 13. Ra5 { and White defends everything - Stohl. } ) 
10. Rxh5 ( { Less promising is } 10. gxh5 $6 Kd6 11. Ke4 Ke6 12. Rc5 Rc8 13. a4 Kd6 14. Kf5 Rb8 $1 { with counterplay for Black. } ) 
Rg8 11. Rh4 $1 ( { Nikolic avoids } 11. Rxh6 $6 Rxg4 12. Rxf6 Rg3 13. Ke4 Rxa3 { , when the draw is not far away. } ) 
Kd7 ( { A counterstrike with } 11... f5 { permits } 12. gxf5 Rg3 13. Ke4 Rxa3 14. f6 $1 { followed by Kf5, when the f6-pawn becomes very dangerous. } ) 
12. Ke4 Ke6 13. Kf3 $1 { Liberating the rook from the defence of the g4-pawn. Thus White doesn't just renew the threat to h6, he also prepares to return the rook to the queenside, where Black's pawns have been rather abandoned by the ... Kc7-d7-e6 manoeuvre. } Rh8 14. Rh5 Rh7 15. Kg3 $1 { A useful move before switching flank with the rook. Once it reaches h5, the king will become a powerful attacking piece. } Rd7 16. Ra5 Ra7 17. Kh4 Kf7 18. Kh5 Kg7 19. f5 $1 ( { Seeing that } 19. Rc5 Rc7 20. a4 $6 { runs into } Rb7 21. Rxc6 Rxb4 22. Rxa6 Rxf4 { Nikolic secures the f-pawn in advance. Meanwhile, Black has no corresponding prophylaxis at his disposal, and must shed a vital tempo. } ) 
Kh7 20. Rc5 Rc7 21. a4 $1 Kg7 22. b5 { White's positional assets are finally converted into material gains. Note the impact of a second front - with the kings on, say, d4 and d7 instead, the plan with b4-b5 wouldn't have been nearly as effective. } axb5 23. axb5 Rb7 24. bxc6 Rc7 25. Rc1 $1 { Zugzwang. The other rook retreats also work, by the way. } Rc8 ( { If } 25... Kh7 { then } 26. Rd1 $1 { is the simplest way to victory; e.g. } Rxc6 27. Rd7 Kg8 28. Kg6 { (winning a tempo thanks to the mating threat - this is the reason why 25 Rc1 was more precise than 25.Rd5) } Kf8 29. Rf7 Ke8 30. Rxf6 Rc4 31. Kh5 -- { followed by } 32. Rxh6 { - Stohl. } ) 
26. c7 Kf7 27. Rc6 ( { Nikolic of course avoids } 27. Kxh6 $4 Rh8 ) 
Kg7 28. Kh4 $1 { Heading for the opposite flank. } Kf7 29. Kg3 ( { After } 29. Kg3 Ke7 30. Kf4 Kf7 31. Ke4 Ke7 32. Kd5 { both } Kf7 33. Kd6 ( { and } 32. Kg3 Kd7 33. Rxf6 { are terminal, so Black resigned. } ) 
 ) 

