 { The next, complex example highlights the usage of zugzwang when working on multiple fronts.Thanks to his more active king and the weak pawn on g4, Black has a clear advantage in this bishop ending. Note that the d5-pawn is hardly a weakness, since it will be ready to advance as soon as the white king leaves the third rank. } 1. { -KEY- } Kd3 { Lobron makes a waiting move with the king, so that the bishop keeps controlling both the g4-pawn and the entry square on e4. } Bb5+ 2. Kd2 ( { After } 2. Kc3 Ba6 { a first zugzwang emerges. The bishop should keep the e4-square under control, but if it moves to g2 or h1, then 3...Be2 follows. Thus White must move his king; e.g. } 3. Kd2 { and after } d4 { similar play arises as in the game. } ) 
d4 { A small triumph for Black - the opponent is further pushed back and a future path along d5-c4 is cleared for the king. } 3. Bb7 { Another waiting move. } ( { a) Here and later, } 3. exd4 $2 Kxd4 { clearly favours Black, whose king now occupies a dominant position, with the b4-pawn in sight. } ) 
 ( { b) On the other hand, after } 3. Bb7 { Black does not benefit from } dxe3 $6 4. Kxe3 { at any moment. As a result, the tension is likely to remain for a while. } ) 
 ( { c) But } 3. Bd1 $5 { was a good alternative, so as to keep the bishop close to the f3-square, and at the same time enable checks at c2 or b3 if Black's king moves to e4 or d5 respectively. There can follow } Ke4 4. Bc2 d3 5. Bb3 Bd7 6. Bd1 $1 Be6 7. Ba4 { (thanks to the previous move, the bishop reaches a more active diagonal) } Kf3 8. Kxd3 Bxg4 9. e4 { White's idea is simple: sacrifice the bishop for the g-pawn in order to leave the opponent with a rook's pawn and the 'wrong' bishop. Black would like avoid this scenario by shepherding the g-pawn with the bishop, but in that case White's e-pawn will be ready to advance; e.g. } Kf2 ( { or } 9. Bb7 Be6 10. Bd1 $1 Kf4 11. Kd4 g4 12. Bxg4 $1 Bxg4 13. Kc3 { followed by Kb2-a1 and White achieves his goal, the pawns on b4 and e4 having no relevance; } ) 
10. e5 Bf5 11. Kd4 g4 12. Bc6 g3 13. Kc5 Bg4 14. Kd6 Bf3 15. Bxf3 Kxf3 16. e6 { will lead to a draw. } ) 
Be8 $1 { Gurevich prepares the following plan: 1. chase away the white bishop from the long diagonal by ...Bg6-e4; 2. transfer his own bishop to the h3-c8 diagonal in order to tie its white colleague to the g4-pawn; 3. place the king on d5. As we will see next, if this plan succeeds then White might end up in zugzwang. } 4. Bf3 Bg6 5. Bb7 $2 { This lazy move seems like the decisive mistake. } ( { Again } 5. Bd1 $1 { was called for; e.g. } Be4 ( { or } 5. Bb7 Bb1 6. Bf3 Ba2 7. Kd3 ) 
6. Ke2 $1 Bc6 7. Kd2 Bb5 ( { if } 7. Bb7 Ke4 { then } 8. Bc2 Kf3 9. Bd1 ) 
8. Bf3 { and Black has not progressed. } ) 
Be4 $1 6. Bc8 ( { a) The pawn ending arising after } 6. Bxe4 $2 Kxe4 7. exd4 Kxd4 { is easily won for Black. } ) 
 ( { b) But } 6. Ba6 { was more tenacious. There can follow } Bc6 7. Be2 ( { if } 7. Bd3 { then } Bd7 8. Be2 Kd5 $1 { with the same zugzwang that later emerges in the game; } ) 
 ( { on the other hand, } 7. Kd3 dxe3 8. Kxe3 Bd7 9. Be2 Kd5 10. Bf1 $1 Bxg4 11. Ba6 { might yield some slight hope of survival, related to a bishop sacrifice for the g-pawn as in the 3 Bd1 subline; } ) 
Ke4 8. Bd3 Kd5 9. Ke2 { White seems to be holding his own; } ( { on } 9. Be2 { there follows the same reply; } ) 
 { but after } Bd7 $1 { Black achieves a similar zugzwang as later on in the game; } 10. Bf5 ( { e.g. } 10. e4 Ke5 11. Kf3 Bc6 12. b5 ( { it is unpleasant to place all the pawns on the 'wrong' colour, but } 12. Bc2 Bb5 13. Bb1 { failed to } Bc4 $1 { followed by d4-d3, and the king enters via d4; } ) 
 { then } Bd7 13. Bc4 Kd6 { and the weakness on b5 decides matters after } 14. Bd3 Kc5 15. e5 Bxb5 16. Bxb5 Kxb5 17. Ke4 Kc5 18. e6 Kd6 ) 
 ( 10. Kf3 $2 dxe3 { drops a pawn; } ) 
Bb5 $1 ( { but not } 10. Bc8 Bxf5 $2 11. gxf5 dxe3 12. b5 $1 { gaining space with a draw; } ) 
11. Kd2 Kc4 $1 { and Black should win; e.g. } 12. Bd3 Kxb4 13. exd4 Bxd3 14. Kxd3 a5 15. Ke4 a4 16. d5 Kc5 $1 { and the a-pawn queens with check. } ) 
Bc6 7. Ba6 ( { a) After } 7. Kd3 Bb5 8. Kd2 Kd5 { Black's king enters decisively via c4 - Gurevich } ) 
 ( { b) While } 7. Bf5 Kd5 8. Bd3 Bd7 9. Be2 { runs into } Bc8 $1 { repeating the zugzwang. } ) 
Bd7 8. Be2 Kd5 $1 { Gurevich has reached his goal. The white bishop must cover both weaknesses: the g4-pawn and the entry square on c4. As it is already performing that task, a waiting move is needed for White. However, there is none available, with the king tied to the e3-pawn. } 9. Bf3+ { The least of evils - Lobron lets the enemy king enter via c4, in exchange for the d4-pawn. } ( 9. Kd3 { failed to } Bb5 { with an easily won pawn ending. The pawn moves aren't advisable either. } ) 
Kc4 10. Be2+ Kxb4 11. exd4 a5 { Black's plan is now simple: advance the a-pawn in order to deflect the white king, and then eliminate the enemy pawns with his own king. } ( 11... Bc6 $6 { was less convincing due to } 12. Ba6 $1 { obstructing the a-pawn. } ) 
12. Kc2 ( { After } 12. Bf3 Kb3 13. d5 ( { or } 13. Kc1 Kc4 14. d5 Kd3 $1 { heading for e4, and the white pawns will soon fall } ) 
Kb2 14. Bd1 a4 15. Bxa4 Bxa4 { Black wins by 16...Bd7 on the next move. } ) 
Bc6 $1 { Heading for the great blockading square on d5. } 13. Kb2 Bd5 14. Bd3 { Lobron resigned at the same time. } ( { There could have followed } 14. Bd3 a4 15. Kc2 a3 16. Bf5 Bb3 17. Kb1 Kc3 { and Black wins comfortably - Gurevich. } ) 

 0-1
